{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: history out of the readable tree. Write notes/\u003cdate\u003e-history-out-of-the-readable-tree.md.\n\nBackground. Josh Rosen's article \"Architecting the 'No Skills' Application\" (X article 2112313639742935040, 2026-10-20) says that every implementation in a repository is a potential example, that deprecated implementations, temporary workarounds and one-off abstractions become patterns an agent discovers and reproduces, and that removing them is essential. Hugh, 2026-10-20: this is definitely important; he believes that readable spikes and retired plans hurt dap directly in the contest evaluation; it applies very sharply to the applications; he wants a design approach. The planner filed this from that conversation.\n\nWhat the tree shows today (main 248aab8f, 2026-10-20).\n- Shipped platform definition versions live in packages/platform/src beside the current one. Version names in the source: destination 1 to 12, register 1 to 17, directory 1 to 16, rules 1 to 10, membership 1 to 8. destination.ts is 2,877 lines, about 1,200 of them versions 1 to 4 (consolidation survey of 2026-10-19 at main dba3e762). 93 test files (19.3k lines) reference only older versions; 30 test files name destination versions 1 to 7. Twelve shared files branch on version lists.\n- Parallel forms: application-install.ts and application-install-wire.ts beside their v2 files (about 600 lines plus tests).\n- Lane generations: apps/room/lanes/src has change.ts and change3.ts, digests.ts and digests3.ts, demo variants, 267 KB of committed canonical JSON and 5.5k test lines.\n- Applications: apps/counter carries definition.json (the first definition, counting, with no runtime user) beside commitments.json. examples/grow carries check/ and demo/ manifests.\n- Notes: 49 dated design notes and 38 plan files (001 to 033) under notes/, plus implementation, reports and reviews. Nothing marks a plan or a note as retired except prose inside it. The three inactive guides are named in one line of docs/README.md. spikes/ is gone from main (no tracked files) but was readable at the contest submission, and scripts/active-source.test.mjs still carries the rule that nothing active reaches spikes/parked/.\n- An agent that searches for how a destination rule is written finds twelve answers. One that reads notes/plans finds thirty-three plans, most of them superseded.\n\nConstraints the note must hold.\n- Replay. A scope pins platform:\u003cname\u003e@\u003cn\u003e at its genesis and is judged and replayed by that version for as long as it exists (packages/platform/src/versions.ts). The journal archive (main b7c54886; A2 and A3 landed) replays recorded journals. Every version that some journal pins must stay executable somewhere.\n- Direction. No backward compatibility (Hugh, 2026-10-04): live rooms at old versions may be re-founded. Hosted rooms today are the rehearsal (dap-lending), the scope Worker (artroom-scope) and grow (dap-grow). Which versions they pin is a question the note answers from the deploy check and the inventory.\n- The consolidation survey (2026-10-19) proposed a replay-only history package and version retirement. Hugh limited that chore to K1 to K3 (helpers, fixtures, custody) and said version retirement was not commissioned. This request commissions the design, not the work.\n\nCandidate approaches the note weighs. It may combine them.\n1. A history home. Versions older than the current one move to a package or an entry that only replay and the versions table import, so the readable src shows one version per definition. Their tests move with them or reduce to replay witnesses.\n2. Retirement. A version that no live scope pins is deleted from source after re-founding; the archive keeps its journals and the history home keeps what replay needs.\n3. A declared active set. A tracked manifest of what is current (packages, applications, definition versions, guides) replaces the regex in active-source.test.mjs. A gate check refuses an active file that imports retired code and refuses a retired path without its marker. A generated index of what is current is the first thing an agent reads.\n4. Notes and plans. A status line in every note and plan (draft, landed, superseded by, retired); retired plans under a retired directory with an index; docs/ is the current description and notes/ is history, said in AGENTS.md and docs/README.md.\n5. Applications. An application directory holds exactly one current definition set. Superseded definitions and lane generations go to the history home with their pinned bytes, or are deleted when nothing pins them. This is the part Hugh called very sharp; it should be able to go first because it needs no compatibility decision.\n6. What a first reader sees. The note says what the root README, docs/ and the package list show after the change, for an evaluator and for an agent.\n\nThe note decides. The disposition of every item above (keep for replay, move, delete, mark); the rule that keeps the tree that way afterwards, as a gate check; the replay invariant and its witness; the question for Hugh on re-founding live rooms, with a recommendation; and slices, sized, in an order where the applications (5) and the notes (4) can go first.\n\nAcceptance. An inventory table of every retired and superseded thing with its disposition; the gate check named; the replay witness named; the slices. Document only. Checker review before landing. The note names no product other than GitHub and cites the article by author and title. Implementation requests follow after it lands.\n\nPriority. Hugh, 2026-10-20: definitely important. Beside the application layout note, above the guard grammar note.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no product names other than GitHub in the note; implementation requests follow after it lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}